# Project Description

## Core features

- Live and historical data in Postgres (ingested via Prefect from EIA)
- Fast database correlating data points on a regional level
- Clean and fun interactive UI for exploring the data (Django + Dash)

## Tech Stack

- Use Python with uv as package manager
- Prefect orchestrator for scheduled ingest jobs (EIA used only by Prefect; no separate REST API for live EIA)
- Postgres SQL database for storing cleaned data
- Django app with Dash (plotly) for dashboarding; data is read from Postgres
- Docker compose setup

## API and Data Service

- Multithreaded API call manager used to retrieve data from external services
- Deposit returned results into postgres database (`ingest`) [Schedule with prefect]
- Run transformations in postgres to generate dashboard tables (`display`) [Schedule with prefect]
- Pull in data at a monthly cadence whenever possible elsewise quarterly or daily

### External Data APIs

- Energy Information Administration (EIA) API:
    - /electricity (use subpath `retail-sales/data` for time-series data rows; without `/data` the API returns dataset metadata only)
    - /natural-gas
    - /petroleum
    - /coal
    - /total-energy

### Ingest and Docker

- Prefect ingest jobs for EIA electricity data: retail-sales (`eia_retail_sales`), electric-power-operational (`eia_electric_power_operational`), state source-disposition (`eia_state_source_disposition`), and state-electricity-profiles/summary (`eia_state_summary`). Each has its own flow and can be scheduled or run on demand. The flow `ingest-eia-electricity-all` runs all four as subflows in one run. No transformation or display jobs in the initial setup.
- Docker Compose runs Postgres, Prefect server, a Prefect process worker, and the Django (web) service on a single machine; see `compose.yaml` and `.env.example`.
- Additional electricity endpoints (e.g. facility-fuel, rto/*) can be added by following the same pattern: migration, db upsert module, flow with fetch + upsert tasks, then wire into the orchestrator and deploy script.

## Django Web App

- Theme is styled off a retro gas station
    - bright reds and teal blues with white background
    - Rounded lines 50s car style shapes
    - Use gas station signs as inspiration for icons
    - A bright daytime theme and a warm but early evening like dark theme
- Theming is implemented with CSS custom properties (day and night themes). Optional styling tech (e.g. Tailwind, Bootstrap) is documented for future use; see `docs/theming.md`.
- Display dashboard (plotly dash) pages with top line selector
    - Show electricity price, generation, consumption for a state over time
        - Have ability to compare neighbors
        - Split by sector if possible
